s we've even narrowed the definition of  what it is to lead a successful life  to lead a successful life now you have  to pass exams when you're young you have  to go to a more or less good university  and you have to have a more or less  successful  usually cognitive biased professional  career  um and  not everybody can or wants to do that  by definition and it will be a disaster  for our economies and societies  everybody did do that  there are vast numbers of things that uh  that  need to be done that don't fall into  that category um  moreover we have in recent decades  created a kind of single funnel  into that zone of  sort of safety and success we've created  a single funnel and that single funnel  is the modern university  uh if you go back 30 40 years there used  to be many more so little that there's  one big ladder up there there used to be  lots of little ladders up  it was possible to get if you hadn't  very well to school but we're pretty  able  uh you could there was promotion from  below is still possible in  in large organizations that has largely  disappeared if you don't have a degree  or even a postgraduate degree you can't  get into those organizations at least  into the into the top ranks of them uh  any longer um and  the system has become self-perpetuating  in a way about 40 percent  of jobs in our society are now graduate  only and the number of them  are growing the proportion of them are  growing um  so you know you know if you are an  anxious parent or a  or a kid 16 17 18  you know of course you're going to see  the path to university as the  as the only one um as the only show in  town really  um you know if you want a decent job  that is now what you have to do  um and um  meanwhile of course um well i mean i i  think we are  we are over producing obviously  we need we need high intelligence um uh  you know it goes without saying and this  is not a book  that is attacking intelligence at all we  need  we need high intelligence we need teams  of highly intelligent people  um you know cooperating across national  boundaries through the internet and so  on to  come up with a vaccine for kobe 19  most immediately but also to to solve  many of our  um technical and environmental problems  you know to find some way of sucking  the atmosphere so um it's not an  argument about that at all but it is an  argument about how we've  we've created um you know outside of the  the sort of  you know the elite band of highly abled  people  across lots of different professions  there is a huge great  sort of um bloated um  cognitive bureaucracy from people of  you know perfectly able people but  people mainly have kind of sort of you  know middling ability or  probably no more bright than most of  their non-graduate peers  and yet it's society is increasingly  designed um for them um  and as i say that this single path  into into higher education is the one  that is most clearly  signposted it's it's simple to  understand it's still  easily the most highly subsidized form  of post-school education  even despite high tuition fees  and meanwhile we have um enormous  skill shortages among skilled trades uh  amongst the  the middling technical functions that  are required to to make the whole system  work you know to make the  boiler in your house work to make  nuclear power stations work to you know  lots of those  middling technician type jobs that don't  necessarily require  a degree but require those sorts of in  the jargon of international education  those are level four level five  technical qualifications um and we're  producing  fewer and fewer people doing those kinds  of qualifications hundreds of thousands  of people used to do hmds and hnc's  every year now it's down to a few tens  of thousands if that  and we also of course have huge um  huge recruitment crisis in in  in care um in care jobs in nursing and  care  um i think 30 40 000  nurse shortages in the nhs uh pride that  was prior to the pandemic and the  pattern when i come onto this later i  think the pandemic  is is nudging us in some of the  directions that i want to see in terms  of  the redistribution of status across  across head hand and heart um so we have  recruitment crisis in nursing even  bigger recruitment crisis in social care  we're going to have to we have a huge  future demand for  specialist dementia nurses and  and currently we're not we're not  meeting those  those needs so we've um we've got out of  kilter  um we've got things that we've we've got  we've got the rewards  to to those clusters of different human  aptitudes out of kilter  now of course like i should also  emphasize  um to talk about head hand and heart  it's of course an abstraction i mean  i mean everything we do is a combination  of of kind of brain and emotion and body  obviously um but i still think it's  quite useful to sort of  as a sort of heuristic device to uh to  abstract and  and and certainly there are jobs that  that draw on  some of those some of those aptitudes  more than others  um so we've got out of kilter  um and it's going to become even worse  in the sense that it turns out that the  knowledge economy doesn't actually  need very many knowledge workers we're  seeing that  even before the real surge  of artificial intelligence artificial  intelligence is  has already arrived around the edges and  lots of different cognitive functions  are now being replaced by you know  automate automation did for blue collar  jobs ai  is increasingly going to do for uh for  at least the kind of middling and lower  level brain  jobs um so so few there will be fewer  jobs there will be fewer head jobs with  real  cognitive content as it were because as  i say the knowledge economy  doesn't actually need that many  knowledge workers and we're seeing that  already  even prior even prior to the  um the arrival of ai on a large scale  we're seeing it in things like the  decline in the  graduate income premium it's down in the  u